5. ALTERNATIVE APPROACH (Hashcat)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
For future reference, Hashcat provides faster GPU-accelerated cracking:

# Clean hash (remove filename)
cut -d':' -f2- pdf1.hash > pdf1_clean.hash

# Crack with Hashcat
hashcat -m 10500 -a 0 pdf1_clean.hash /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt

# Show cracked password
hashcat -m 10500 --show pdf1_clean.hash

6. SECURITY ANALYSIS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6.1 Password Strength Assessment
--------------------------------
Cracked passwords reveal:
[ANALYZE PASSWORDS - e.g.,]
- Common dictionary words
- Short length (< 8 characters)
- No special characters
- Easily guessable patterns

6.2 Vulnerabilities Identified
------------------------------
- PDF encryption is only as strong as the password
- Dictionary attacks effectively crack weak passwords
- Default wordlists contain common password patterns
- No rate limiting or lockout mechanisms in PDF encryption

6.3 Recommendations
-------------------
For securing PDF files:
1. Use passwords with > 12 characters
2. Include uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols
3. Avoid dictionary words or common patterns
4. Use passphrases instead of passwords
5. Consider using password managers for complex passwords
6. Implement additional encryption layers if possible

7. LESSONS LEARNED
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Password security relies heavily on password complexity
- Default wordlists are effective against common passwords
- PDF encryption is vulnerable to offline dictionary attacks
- Time-based attacks are limited by computing power and wordlist size
- Understanding attack methodologies helps in defense strategies

